    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under corporate finance theory, WACC is the market-value-weighted average of the after-tax component costs. Total market value V = $80M + $12M + $48M = $140M. Equity weight: $80M/$140M = 0.5714 × 13.00% = 7.43%. Preferred weight: $12M/$140M = 0.0857 × 9.00% = 0.77%. Debt weight: $48M/$140M = 0.3429 × 7.00% × (1 − 0.25) = 0.3429 × 5.25% = 1.80%. WACC = 7.43% + 0.77% + 1.80% = 10.00%. The debt component uses the after-tax cost because interest is tax-deductible (IRC §163), reducing the effective cost from 7.00% to 5.25%. Market values (not book values) are used for weights because they represent the current economic cost of each capital source. This 10.00% WACC is the discount rate for NPV analysis of average-risk projects.",
