      "A": "Under MM, capital structure is completely irrelevant regardless of taxes. Adding debt changes the cost of equity exactly enough to offset the benefit of cheaper debt, leaving WACC unchanged at 11.00%. The board should not waste time on capital structure decisions — they have no effect on firm value in any tax regime.",
      "B": "Under MM with taxes (1963), adding debt creates value through the interest tax shield. The levered firm value VL = VU + t × D, where t × D is the present value of the perpetual tax savings. With $100M equity and 35% debt, the tax shield adds 28% × $35M debt-equivalent of value. The WACC declines from 11.00% (unlevered) to a lower levered rate because interest payments reduce taxable income. In contrast, MM without taxes (1958) would predict no value change — the tax shield is the source of value creation.",
