    "CognitiveLevel": "Apply",
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate",
    "DifficultyScore": 3,
    "ExplanationCorrect": "A forward hedge eliminates transaction exposure by locking in today the exchange rate at which future foreign-currency-denominated payables or receivables will be settled. Forward hedge cost = Forward rate × Foreign currency amount = $1.135/€ × €2,000,000 = $2,270,000 in 60 days. A money market hedge replicates the forward contract synthetically: (1) buy the present value of €2,000,000 today at the euro borrowing rate, (2) invest those euros to grow to exactly €2,000,000 in 60 days to meet the payable, and (3) finance the euro purchase with a dollar loan. Under covered interest rate parity, the forward rate = Spot × (1 + r_domestic) / (1 + r_foreign), so the two methods should produce equivalent costs in efficient markets. Systematic differences arise only from transaction costs or deviations from parity. The forward hedge has the advantage of simplicity — one transaction fixes the exchange rate with certainty. The money market hedge involves multiple transactions (borrowing, converting, investing) and may have higher transaction costs.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Forward hedges are not always cheaper. In efficiently functioning markets, the forward rate incorporates the interest rate differential between the two currencies (covered interest rate parity). The forward hedge and money market hedge should produce equivalent net costs. The forward hedge is simpler, but not inherently cheaper. In some cases, transaction costs (bid-ask spreads on FX, borrowing spreads) may make one method marginally more expensive than the other, but there is no systematic cost advantage to forward contracts.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Remaining unhedged exposes Bridgeway to significant transaction exposure. If the euro strengthens to $1.18/€ during the 60-day period, the dollar cost increases from approximately $2,270,000 to $2,360,000 — a $90,000 unfavorable variance. The historical strength of the euro provides no guarantee about future exchange rate movements. For a corporation with a defined payable or receivable in a foreign currency, leaving the position unhedged is equivalent to speculating on currency movements — which is not the proper role of a corporate treasury. The purpose of hedging is to remove uncertainty, not to generate trading profits.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Money market hedges are not 'redundant' simply because IRP links forward rates to interest rates. In practice, the forward market and money markets are complementary — both achieve the same economic result. The money market hedge is useful when forward markets for a particular currency or maturity are illiquid, or when the firm already has borrowing/investment relationships that make money market transactions cheaper. Neither method is always dominant; the choice depends on market conditions and transaction costs.",
