  "UniqueConceptKey": "B-266-capm-cost-of-equity-single-factor-beta-estimation",
  "Stem": "Meridian Dynamics, a mid-cap automation supplier, is preparing its annual capital budgeting hurdle rate. CFO Dana Lee has gathered market data for the CAPM estimate: the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield is 3.80% as the risk-free rate, the expected market portfolio return is 11.20%, and Meridian's levered equity beta is 1.25. The finance team must apply CAPM correctly to determine the cost of equity that will anchor the WACC for evaluating a $45 million expansion. Which CAPM result correctly reflects Meridian's systematic risk?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "17.80% — calculated as 3.80% + 1.25 × 11.20%, applying beta to the full market return instead of the premium",
    "B": "13.05% — calculated as 3.80% + 1.25 × (11.20% − 3.80%) = 3.80% + 1.25 × 7.40% = 3.80% + 9.25%",
    "C": "9.25% — calculated as 1.25 × (11.20% − 3.80%) = 1.25 × 7.40%, omitting the risk-free base compensation",
    "D": "9.72% — calculated as 3.80% + (11.20% − 3.80%) / 1.25 = 3.80% + 5.92%, dividing the premium by beta"
  },
  "CorrectChoice": "B",
  "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the Capital Asset Pricing Model (Sharpe, 1964; Lintner, 1965), CAPM defines the cost of equity as R_e = R_f + β(R_m − R_f). The market risk premium is R_m − R_f = 11.20% − 3.80% = 7.40%, the compensation for bearing systematic risk. Multiplying by beta: 1.25 × 7.40% = 9.25% risk premium for Meridian's 25% above-market systematic risk. Adding the risk-free base: 3.80% + 9.25% = 13.05%. Business interpretation: Meridian must earn at least 13.05% on equity-financed projects to satisfy investors given its beta; using this as the equity component prevents accepting projects that destroy shareholder value. Common trap: applying beta to the full market return (11.20%) double-counts the risk-free rate already embedded in R_m, inflating the estimate to 17.80%.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "This choice applies beta to the full market return (11.20%) rather than the risk premium (7.40%). That double-counts the 3.80% risk-free rate — once as the stand-alone base and again inside the market return. The correct term is β(R_m − R_f), not β×R_m. A candidate using this path inflates the cost of equity by 1.25 × 3.80% = 4.75 percentage points.",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "This calculation derives the risk premium correctly (9.25%) but omits the risk-free rate. CAPM compensates investors for time value (R_f) plus systematic risk exposure. An equity investor requires 3.80% even for a zero-beta asset; Meridian's shareholders need both components. Omitting R_f understates the hurdle by the full Treasury yield.",
  "ExplanationWrongD": "Dividing the premium by beta inverts the risk adjustment. Beta scales the premium upward for aggressive stocks (β > 1) and downward for defensive stocks (β < 1). Meridian is aggressive at 1.25, so its premium must exceed 7.40%, not fall to 5.92%. This distractor captures a candidate who remembers beta adjusts the premium but misapplies the operation.",
