  "CorrectChoice": "C",
  "ExplanationCorrect": "Corporate finance theory and IRC §163 require the after-tax cost of debt to use the yield to maturity (YTM), not the historical coupon rate, because YTM is the market-required return at which Hawthorne could issue new debt today. After-tax cost = R_d × (1 − t) = 7.20% × (1 − 0.24) = 7.20% × 0.76 = 5.472%, rounded to 5.47%. The 24% tax shield reflects that each dollar of interest saves $0.24 in taxes, so the government funds nearly a quarter of the borrowing cost. Using the 5.90% coupon understates the true marginal cost by 0.98 percentage points because the bonds were priced when rates were lower. This 5.47% enters WACC as the debt component; a candidate using the coupon would understate WACC and overstate project NPVs.",
