  "ExplanationWrongB": "The 5.90% coupon is the historical rate locked at issuance and does not reflect Hawthorne's current borrowing cost. Because market rates have risen, the bonds trade at a discount and investors now demand 7.20%. WACC measures the opportunity cost of new capital, not the cost of old debt. Using the coupon understates the marginal cost by 5.47% − 4.49% = 0.98 percentage points and would inflate project NPVs.",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "",
  "ExplanationWrongD": "The tax rate is given as 24% marginal, not 28%. The correct factor is 0.76, not 0.72. While 5.18% results from applying the right formula to the wrong rate, Hawthorne's WACC must reflect its actual marginal rate — the rate on the next dollar of taxable income. Using 28% overstates the shield and understates the debt cost by 0.29 percentage points.",
